# Test policy

This page explains the verification layers used by Neon Pairs and the kinds of failures each layer can establish.

The repository checks rule transitions, serialized input, source conventions and complete browser journeys. These checks share documented examples but do not all run in the same environment. `npm test` runs Vitest; `npm run test:e2e` builds the current application and diagnostic entries, then uses Node's test runner with Playwright browser support.

## Focused evidence stays near behavior

The core has adjacent tests such as `game.test.ts`, `deck.test.ts`, `snapshot.test.ts` and `replay.test.ts`. Their names make the behavior under discussion easy to locate before reading the whole suite. A feature's rules.ts has a rules.test.ts beside it. Complete interaction journeys remain at the application test level, where focus, storage, browser lifecycle and delivery can be observed together.

Adjacency is useful because a transition and its evidence can be read in one pass. It does not mean every production file in the repository has a one-to-one test file. Some concerns have several focused files: completion, move limits, snapshot selection and replay ordering each get separate attention. Other helpers are exercised through a broader behavior or browser journey.

The architecture specification A10 asks for focused tests near behavior, independent literal generation vectors, transitions checked against stated rules and built HTML checked from disk as well as served. Those are different obligations because no single test environment can establish all of them.

## What Vitest includes

`vitest.config.ts` includes `src/**/*.test.ts` and `scripts/**/*.test.ts`. It does not include the browser files in `tests`, which end in `.e2e.test.ts` and run under the separate command. There is no hidden browser suite behind `npm test`.

Most unit tests use `node:assert/strict` with `test` imported from Vitest. They call pure functions directly. Clock arithmetic receives numerical observations; session transitions receive a session object; preference tests pass a memory or failing store. This makes failures specific: a mismatch boundary can be checked without waiting for a real timeout.

The script tests also run here. `replay.test.ts` launches child processes to exercise command output and failure exits, and the source-audit files read authored files and configuration. The CLI test has a 60-second timeout because child processes can be slow on a busy verifier. That allowance is test scheduling, not a game timing constant.

## Independence for generated data

A generator test that computes its expected deck using the same generator can prove little about compatibility. The repository instead contains a literal example deck for `np1-4-1`, initial recurrence values and independent rejection vectors. Browser support also has `specDeal` in `tests/deal-oracle.ts`, which expresses the shuffle with BigInt arithmetic.

The rejection tests cover both a draw above the accepted limit and a draw exactly at it. That equality case matters because a one-character comparison change would alter the seeded result. Completion tests iterate …28 tokens truncated…/game/Game.tsx` composes the screen. A page about a turn can begin in the core and follow only the layers relevant to its question.

The same discipline helps avoid false shortcuts. Saying “React counts a move on click” combines rendering, input and rules into one inaccurate sentence. A second eligible activation increments the counter in `activateCard`; browser controls route input to the shared action. That description also explains why keyboard and pointer input have the same move behavior.

## Examples answer a particular question

The code `np1-4-1` is a good common example because the specification publishes its deck. Positions 0 and 11 form one pair, while positions 0 and 1 differ. Those facts let a writer explain a match or a miss without depending on an unknown fresh deal. A precise example identifies whether its numbers are positions, face IDs or human rows and columns.

A snapshot example answers a state question: what progress will load? A replay example answers a sequence question: which activations and timing lead to a result? Substituting one for the other can omit the very detail a report needs. [Sharing FAQ](../sharing/sharing-faq.md) explains the choice, while the format pages define acceptable data.

Complete format examples can be copied and checked. Fragments can be clearer when discussing just one branch, provided the prose identifies them as fragments. An object showing only `kind` and `index` is a selection example, not an entire snapshot. Calling it a snapshot would imply that the decoder accepts missing top-level fields.

Placeholders also need context. In a sentence explaining a completion message, `N` can stand for a move count. Inside a JSON block intended as valid input, an unquoted `N` is not a valid number. The difference is about the example's purpose, rather than a preference for more or less code on the page.

## Explain the boundary that changes the answer

“The cards close after a delay” is enough in a quick introduction. An engineering explanation needs the active-time distinction because a hidden page or paused import changes when the interval finishes. “The game saves locally” needs a similar boundary: preferences and best moves persist, while ongoing progress is restored through explicit snapshot tools.

There is no need to repeat the complete timing or persistence model every time those subjects appear. A sentence can name the relevant condition and link to [Mismatch interval](../gameplay/mismatch-interval.md) or [Persistence overview](../persistence/persistence-overview.md). Repetition is particularly risky for numeric limits and exact strings, which can become inconsistent when copied into many pages.

The best edge case is one that clarifies the rule being explained. Repeating the first revealed card demonstrates why activation is not the same as a move. A stale timer after Restart demonstrates why session generation exists. An unsupported side length demonstrates board-code validation. Adding an unrelated browser limitation to each of those examples would distract from the point.

## Evidence has a scope

Reading `formatElapsed` establishes how the formatter computes text. A browser test establishes how the status component exposes that value in a built page. A screen-reader journey involves another layer of evidence. These observations support different claims and do not automatically stand in for one another.

The repository README records a named runtime combination and says that spoken screen-reader output was not run. That is a useful model of a bounded evidence statement: it reports what was checked and leaves the unperformed part visible. A documentation edit alone does not supply a new test run or expand the tested browser matrix.

For command instructions, `package.json` is the source for the available scripts. `npm run test:e2e` builds before executing the browser journeys; `npm run build` type checks before Vite emits the HTML. Describing the command's defined behavior is different from claiming it passed in the writer's environment. [Test strategy](../quality/test-strategy.md) covers the larger evidence plan.
